# Line endings are LF in the repository and LF in every working tree.
#
# Not a preference, and not cosmetic. `.prettierrc` pins `endOfLine: "lf"` and
# `pnpm check` runs `prettier --check`, so a checkout that converts to CRLF
# fails the gate on every file in the repo. GitHub's `windows-latest` runners
# ship Git for Windows with `core.autocrlf=true` — which is exactly that
# checkout — so the Windows probe job in `ci.yml` cannot go green without this
# file, and would go red for a reason that says nothing about Windows.
#
# `eol=lf` rather than a bare `text=auto`: `auto` normalises what is *stored*
# and then leaves the working tree to `core.autocrlf`, which is the setting
# causing the problem. `eol=lf` overrides it.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Binary, so never inspected for line endings and never diffed as text.
#
# Git infers most of these correctly from content. Stating them means a corrupt
# icon or an unloadable native module can never be blamed on a heuristic, and
# the Windows artefacts further down are ones no macOS checkout has seen yet.
*.png binary
*.svg text
*.icns binary
*.ico binary
*.node binary
*.dll binary
*.exe binary
*.pdb binary
*.vsix binary

# The one exception to LF, and it only arrives with the NSIS work in Phase 5.
#
# `cmd.exe` reads a batch file a line at a time and has documented failure modes
# with LF-only endings around labels and `goto`, and the NSIS compiler is
# stricter still. Neither is worth discovering from a broken installer, so the
# rule is stated before the first such file exists.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf
*.nsi text eol=crlf
*.nsh text eol=crlf
